# The toolchain, in one place. `mix kiln.toolchain.check` fails when the
# Dockerfile's ARGs or mix.exs's `elixir:` requirement disagree with this file,
# and CI's setup-beam reads it directly (version-file:) rather than restating it.
#
# Exact versions on purpose. CI used to pin "1.19"/"27", which setup-beam
# resolves to whatever is newest at the time — so the toolchain CI tested on
# moved on its own, and could not be matched by the Dockerfile, which needs
# exact tags. See #600.
#
# Floor: Elixir must stay 1.19.3+. config/dev.exs uses the `E` (`:export`) regex
# modifier on its live-reload patterns, which 1.18 cannot parse (#572).
#
# Changing these is a real change: bump, run `mix kiln.toolchain.check`, mirror
# into the Dockerfile ARGs, and expect dialyzer to have opinions on an OTP major
# bump (#599). setup-beam matches these strictly, so a version with no published
# build for ubuntu-24.04 fails every job — check builds.hex.pm first.
#
# OTP 27 is a deliberate floor, not a stale pin (#600). CI and the release image
# run what this file says; a developer's machine may be further ahead, and today
# typically is. The cost of that gap is specific and worth stating rather than
# rediscovering: OTP 29's dialyzer performs stricter opacity checking than 27's,
# so that class of warning is invisible to CI and is caught only by whoever runs
# `mix dialyzer` locally — which is exactly how #599 got in and stayed green.
#
# It is a floor rather than a match because this file also decides what
# *production* runs: the release image should run the toolchain the test suite
# and dialyzer actually ran against, so moving it is a deploy-affecting change
# and belongs in its own PR, with #599's class cleared first so the bump is not
# noisy.
elixir 1.19.5-otp-27
erlang 27.3.4.15
